# Every byte in this repository is either hashed, signed, or read as a
# specification. Git must never rewrite any of it: a checkout that converts
# line endings changes a payload's hash, invalidates a signature, or changes
# the size of the very file a reader is asked to fingerprint.
* -text

# The verifier and its specification are pinned to LF explicitly, mirroring
# `verifier/** text eol=lf` in the repository that produces them. Without that
# rule, a Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true` published a CRLF `verify.mjs`
# next to an LF `README.md` — two different byte counts for a program whose
# whole point is that two readers can compare its fingerprint and get the same
# answer.
verify.mjs text eol=lf
README.md text eol=lf

# Timestamp tokens are DER, not text.
*.tsr binary
